A = [
dict(n=2, slug="tokens", title="How Does an AI Read a Word?", topic="Tokens",
buzz='"This model has a 200,000-token limit." "We pay per million tokens." Tokens are on every AI bill and every spec sheet. Most people quietly assume a token is a word. It isn\'t.',
hw='Give an AI the word "unbelievable". It doesn\'t see one word. It might see three pieces: **un**, **believ**, **able**.\n\nThat\'s the whole idea. An AI reads text as small pieces it already knows, like LEGO bricks. Common words are often one brick ("the", "cat"). Rare or long words are built from several. Even a space or a comma can be its own piece.',
step='Before training, the AI\'s makers build a fixed "brick set": a vocabulary of tens of thousands of pieces, chosen because they appear often in text. Every sentence you type is cut into those bricks, and each brick gets a number. The AI only ever works with the numbers.\n\nA rough rule of thumb: in English, one token is about four letters, so 100 words is roughly 130 to 150 tokens. Many other languages, and code, need more tokens for the same meaning.',
met='Every AI price list ("per million tokens"), every "message too long" error, and every time an AI miscounted the letters in a word. It sees bricks, not letters.',
name='**Tokens**, made by a **tokenizer**.',
words=[("Token","one piece of text the AI reads, often part of a word."),("Tokenizer","the tool that cuts text into tokens."),("Vocabulary","the fixed set of pieces a model knows.")],
notit='A token is not a word, and not a letter. Two AIs can split the same sentence differently, so "1,000 tokens" isn\'t the same amount of text everywhere.',
take=["When you see a token limit or price, translate it: roughly three-quarters of a word per token in English.","Budget more tokens for Hindi, Tamil, Japanese or code.","Ask your vendor which tokenizer they use before comparing prices."],
close="An AI doesn't read words. It reads bricks.",
q="Did you think a token was a word?",
scene='"unbelievable" is one word to you.\nTo an AI, it might be three: un, believ, able.',
hook="An AI doesn't read words. It reads pieces, called tokens.",
extra="That's why AI prices and limits are counted in tokens, not words."),

dict(n=3, slug="how-llm-writes", title="How Does an AI Write a Sentence?", topic="How an LLM writes",
buzz='"Large language model." It sounds like a giant brain composing essays. The real trick is much smaller, and repeated very fast.',
hw='Finish this: "The cat sat on the ___".\n\nYou thought "mat". So would an AI. That\'s the hello world: an AI writes by guessing the **next token**, one at a time.\n\nIt guesses "mat". Then it looks at "The cat sat on the mat" and guesses the next piece: maybe ".". Then the next. A whole essay is that one guess, repeated hundreds of times.',
step='The AI doesn\'t pick one answer. For every next piece it gives each option a score: "mat" very likely, "sofa" likely, "moon" unlikely. Then one is picked, and the loop continues.\n\nIt learned those scores by reading a huge amount of text and practising this exact game: hide the next word, guess it, get corrected, billions of times.',
met='Your phone keyboard suggesting the next word. Same idea, far smaller. And the way ChatGPT or Claude types its answer bit by bit on screen: you\'re watching the guesses happen.',
name='A **large language model (LLM)**, doing **next-token prediction**.',
words=[("LLM","a model trained to predict the next token in text."),("Next-token prediction","guessing the most likely next piece, again and again."),("Probability","the score the AI gives each possible next piece.")],
notit='It\'s not looking answers up in a database, and it doesn\'t plan the whole paragraph first. That\'s why it can sound fluent and still be wrong (more in write-up 30).',
take=["Watch an AI answer appear word by word: that's next-token prediction live.","Remember fluent isn't the same as correct.","Ask: what text would make the right next words most likely? That's what a good prompt does."],
close="One guess, repeated very fast. That's how an AI writes.",
q="Did you know your phone keyboard works on the same idea?",
scene='"The cat sat on the ___"\nYou said "mat". So would an AI.',
hook="An LLM writes by guessing the next word, again and again.",
extra="A whole essay is that one guess, repeated hundreds of times."),

dict(n=4, slug="embeddings", title="How Does an AI Know \"King\" Is Close to \"Queen\"?", topic="Embeddings",
buzz='"We\'ll embed the documents and store the vectors." Embeddings power AI search, recommendations and RAG. The word sounds like maths homework.',
hw='Draw a map. Put "king" on it. Put "queen" right next to it. Put "banana" far away, near "apple".\n\nThat\'s the hello world: an **embedding** is a word\'s position on a map of meaning. Similar meanings sit close together. Different meanings sit far apart.',
step='A real map has two directions: north and east. An AI\'s meaning map has hundreds or thousands of directions at once, so it can place things by many qualities: royalty, gender, food, formality.\n\nEach position is just a long list of numbers. To ask "how similar are these two things?", the AI measures how close their positions are. It works for sentences, pages and images too, not just single words.',
met='Searching "cheap flights" and finding "budget airfares". Spotify suggesting songs that feel similar. Photo apps finding "dog" pictures you never labelled.',
name='**Embeddings**, also called **vectors**.',
words=[("Embedding","a list of numbers that places something on a map of meaning."),("Vector","that list of numbers."),("Similarity search","finding the items closest on the map.")],
notit='An embedding isn\'t a definition or a keyword. Two texts can share no words and still sit close together. That\'s the point. (How this powers document search is covered in #JustAddRAG.)',
take=["When someone says \"semantic search\", picture the meaning map.","Ask what was embedded: words, sentences or whole pages? It changes results.","Remember: close on the map means similar meaning, not true."],
close="Embeddings turn meaning into distance.",
q="Where have you seen search that understood what you meant?",
scene='Put "king" on a map.\n"Queen" lands right next to it. "Banana" lands far away.',
hook="An embedding is a word's address on a map of meaning.",
extra="Close on the map means close in meaning."),

dict(n=5, slug="attention", title="How Does an AI Know What \"It\" Means?", topic="Attention",
buzz='"Transformers." "Attention is all you need." The idea behind almost every modern AI sounds like a sci-fi film.',
hw='Read this: "The trophy didn\'t fit in the suitcase because **it** was too big."\n\nWhat\'s "it"? The trophy. You worked it out by glancing back at the other words. That glance is the hello world: **attention**. For every word, the AI looks at all the other words and decides which ones matter for understanding it.',
step='Change one word: "...because it was too **small**." Now "it" is the suitcase. Attention catches that, because "small" pulls the focus to a different word.\n\nA transformer does this for every word at once, in many layers, each looking for different links: who did what, what refers to what, which words go together. Older AIs read one word at a time and forgot the start of long sentences. Attention lets every word see every other word directly.',
met='Every modern chatbot and translator. Translation got much better around 2017 to 2018, when this design took over.',
name='**Attention**, the core of the **transformer**.',
words=[("Attention","each word deciding which other words to focus on."),("Transformer","the AI design built on attention, the \"T\" in GPT."),("Layer","one round of attention; models stack many.")],
notit='Attention isn\'t the AI paying attention the way you do, and it isn\'t memory of past chats. It works only within the text in front of it right now (that\'s the context window, write-up 6).',
take=["Next time you hear \"transformer\", picture every word glancing at every other word.","Remember why long texts cost more: every word looks at every other word.","Know that GPT stands for Generative Pre-trained Transformer."],
close="Every word looks at every other word. That's attention.",
q="Did you get the trophy and suitcase right on the first try?",
scene='"The trophy didn\'t fit in the suitcase because it was too big."\nWhat\'s "it"?',
hook="You glanced back at the other words. So does an AI. That's attention.",
extra="It's the idea behind almost every modern AI."),

dict(n=6, slug="context-window", title="Why Does an AI Forget the Start of a Long Chat?", topic="Context window",
buzz='"This model has a one-million-token context window." It\'s on every launch announcement. Bigger sounds better. But what is it?',
hw='Picture a desk. Everything the AI can see right now has to fit on it: your question, the chat so far, any documents you pasted, and its answer.\n\nThe desk has a fixed size. When it\'s full, something has to come off, usually the oldest pages. That\'s the hello world: the **context window** is the AI\'s desk.',
step='The desk is measured in tokens (write-up 2). Everything counts: your messages, its replies, hidden instructions, pasted files.\n\nMost chat apps quietly drop or summarise old messages when the desk fills up. That\'s why a long chat can "forget" what you said at the start. Bigger desks help, but AIs can still miss details buried in the middle of a very long desk, and bigger desks cost more per question.',
met='A long chat where the AI forgets your name or the rules you set at the start. Or a "this document is too long" error.',
name='The **context window**, measured in **tokens**.',
words=[("Context window","the most text an AI can consider at once."),("Truncation","cutting older text to make room."),("Long context","models with very large windows.")],
notit='The context window isn\'t memory. When the chat ends, the desk is cleared. Remembering you across chats is a separate feature (covered in Series 12, memory).',
take=["Put the most important instructions and facts near your question, not buried in the middle.","Start a fresh chat for a new topic.","Remember: a bigger desk can still be messy. Fewer, better documents often beat more."],
close="The AI only knows what's on its desk right now.",
q="Has an AI ever forgotten something you told it earlier?",
scene='You told the AI your rules at the start of the chat.\nAn hour later, it ignored them.',
hook="An AI's context window is its desk. When it's full, something falls off.",
extra="And when the chat ends, the desk is cleared."),

dict(n=7, slug="temperature", title="Why Does the Same Question Get Different Answers?", topic="Temperature",
buzz='"Set temperature to zero." Developers say it like a magic spell. What is being cooled down?',
hw='Ask an AI to finish "My favourite fruit is ___" five times.\n\nIt might say apple, mango, apple, banana, apple. Remember write-up 3: the AI scores every possible next word, then **picks** one. Temperature is how adventurous that pick is.',
step='**Low temperature:** almost always pick the top-scoring word. Answers are steady and predictable. Good for facts, forms and code.\n\n**High temperature:** give lower-scoring words a real chance. Answers vary and surprise. Good for brainstorming, stories and taglines.\n\nEven at temperature zero, answers can still differ slightly, and other changes (a new model version, different wording) change answers too.',
met='Pressing "regenerate" and getting a different answer. Creative tools that offer "more creative" or "more precise" modes.',
name='**Temperature**, a **sampling** setting.',
words=[("Sampling","choosing the next word from the AI's scored options."),("Temperature","how much randomness goes into that choice."),("Deterministic","same input, same output, every time.")],
notit='Temperature doesn\'t make the AI smarter or more accurate. Low temperature makes it consistent, even consistently wrong. (Why answers change after a model update is in #JustAddRAG 14.)',
take=["Use low temperature for anything that must be repeatable.","Use higher temperature when you want options, then pick.","Don't treat temperature zero as a guarantee of identical answers."],
close="Temperature decides how adventurous the next word is.",
q="Do you want your AI predictable or creative?",
scene='Ask an AI "My favourite fruit is ___" five times.\nApple. Mango. Apple. Banana. Apple.',
hook="Temperature is how adventurous the AI's next word is.",
extra="Low for facts, high for ideas."),

dict(n=8, slug="parameters", title="What Does \"70B\" Actually Mean?", topic="Parameters",
buzz='"A 70B model." "An 8B model." Model names come with numbers, and bigger numbers sound smarter. What is being counted?',
hw='Picture a mixing desk in a music studio, covered in tiny knobs. Turn the knobs one way and the song sounds muddy. Turn them right and it sounds great.\n\nAn AI is a mixing desk with billions of knobs. **70B means 70 billion knobs.** Training is the process of turning every knob, a tiny bit at a time, until the AI\'s next-word guesses (write-up 3) come out right.',
step='Each knob is a number, called a **parameter** or **weight**. Nobody sets them by hand. During training, the AI guesses, sees how wrong it was, and every knob is nudged slightly to be less wrong next time, over and over across a huge amount of text.\n\nMore knobs can hold more patterns, but they also need more memory and more computing power to run.',
met='Model names like "Llama 3 70B" or "8B". When an AI runs on your phone, it\'s usually one with a few billion knobs, not hundreds of billions.',
name='**Parameters**, also called **weights**.',
words=[("Parameter","one adjustable number inside the model."),("Weights","all those numbers together; the model's learned knowledge."),("Model size","how many parameters it has, like 8B or 70B.")],
notit='More parameters isn\'t automatically better. Newer small models often beat older big ones, because training data and methods matter as much as size (write-up 14).',
take=["Read \"70B\" as 70 billion learned knobs, not 70 billion facts.","Ask what size you actually need: smaller is cheaper and faster.","Compare models on your own tasks, not on size alone."],
close="70B means 70 billion knobs, tuned by reading.",
q="Did you think bigger always meant smarter?",
scene='"We\'re using a 70B model."\nSeventy billion what?',
hook="70B means 70 billion parameters: tiny knobs, each tuned during training.",
extra="More knobs can hold more, but cost more to run."),

dict(n=9, slug="training-vs-inference", title="Is the AI Learning While You Chat With It?", topic="Training vs inference",
buzz='"Training" and "inference" appear in every AI budget, chip announcement and data policy. Most people assume the AI learns from every chat. Usually, it doesn\'t.',
hw='A student spends years at school. That\'s **training**: slow, expensive, done once.\n\nThen the student sits an exam and answers one question. That\'s **inference**: quick, cheap, done millions of times.\n\nWhen you chat with an AI, it\'s sitting the exam. It isn\'t back at school.',
step='Training turns the knobs (write-up 8) using huge amounts of text and computing power, over weeks or months. Then the knobs are frozen and the model is released.\n\nEvery question you ask is inference: the frozen model makes its guesses. It doesn\'t change its knobs because of your chat. A company may later use chats (if its policy allows) to train the **next** version, but that\'s a separate, later step.',
met='Chip news: some chips are built for training, others for inference. Privacy settings that ask "may we use your chats to improve our models?" That\'s about future training.',
name='**Training** and **inference**.',
words=[("Training","teaching the model by adjusting its knobs on lots of data."),("Inference","using the trained, frozen model to answer."),("Knowledge cutoff","the date its training data ends.")],
notit='Inference isn\'t learning. If an AI seems to remember you, that\'s a separate memory feature or the chat history on its desk (write-up 6), not new training.',
take=["Remember the AI's knowledge stops at its training cutoff unless it can search.","Check your vendor's policy on using your data for future training.","Expect most of your AI bill to be inference, paid per question."],
close="School is training. Every answer is an exam.",
q="Did you think the AI was learning from your chats?",
scene='You corrected the AI yesterday.\nToday it makes the same mistake.',
hook="Training is school. Chatting is the exam. The AI isn't learning mid-exam.",
extra="Your chat doesn't change the model's knobs."),
]
